erspective and say  well there are many many uh ways to  generate energy  um but there is practically only one  viable way to make  organic carbon out of uh co2 that's  photosynthesis  so as as much as we can keeping  co2 in an organic form and creating  value on earth  for humanity is a better way to to do  than  returning it as co2 even if we offset  some other  um non-desirable thing so that's sort of  my more philosophical geopolitical view  on that  why i would if possible always favor  keeping more  more organic carbon on earth  thank you thanks dominic  johannes peter you also want to make a  comment about  uh n in in methane  yes very briefly i just want to say um  because the popular press focuses so  much on carbon  uh it's really important to remember  that in agriculture  greenhouse gas emissions are entirely  dominated by nitrous oxide and methane  and that's totally different from any  other sector so at the global scale  those are are a small part of the issue  but if you're interested in agriculture  and greenhouse gas mitigation in  agriculture  you absolutely have to pay attention to  methane  and nitrous oxide and that's important  because  everything's not always a win-win and of  course carbon and nitrogen are  are stoichiometrically linked in soil  and elsewhere  so you know we're talking about carbon  and nitrogen cycling and and that's  important i think  uh to remember and and in terms of  focusing on  on solutions and avoiding uh problems  disguised as solutions  excellent thank you peter um we are 10  minutes over time now so i think we  should  officially close but i really like to  thank the panel it was fascinating  discussion  and i'm hoping you'll consent to do a  spring follow-on because i suspect  that we could talk about this for a full  day and only start to scratch the  surface  so thank you so much everybody it's a  it's been a delight to have you put the  panel together  and thanks everybody else for attending  see you next wee